CART OPERATOR ALGEBRA
=====================
A coordinate‑free algebra relational tensioner
CART = (C, K, D, C/K, H)

This algebra defines the operators, compositions, identities,
and interaction laws between the five components.


------------------------------------------------------------
0. OPERATOR SET
------------------------------------------------------------
The CART operator algebra consists of:

    Id      : identity operator
    K       : kernel (tension-resolution operator)
    D       : defect (residual tension operator)
    Q       : quotient constructor (identity formation)
    H       : hierarchy operator (entanglement depth)

Composite operators:
    KD      : defect-after-resolution
    QK      : quotient-after-resolution
    HQ      : hierarchy-after-quotient
    HK      : hierarchy-after-resolution
    HD      : hierarchy-after-defect


------------------------------------------------------------
1. IDENTITY OPERATOR
------------------------------------------------------------

(Id1) Identity
    Id(x) = x

(Id2) Left Identity
    Id ∘ K = K

(Id3) Right Identity
    K ∘ Id = K

(Id4) Idempotence
    Id ∘ Id = Id


------------------------------------------------------------
2. KERNEL OPERATOR (K)
------------------------------------------------------------

(K1) Endomorphism
    K : C → C

(K2) Idempotence
    K ∘ K = K

(K3) Contractive Law
    D(K(x)) ≤ D(x)

(K4) Canonical Representative
    K(x) ∈ Q(x)

(K5) Kernel Fixed Points
    K(x) = x  ⇔  x is canonical


------------------------------------------------------------
3. DEFECT OPERATOR (D)
------------------------------------------------------------

(D1) Defect Definition
    D(x) = δ(x, K(x))

(D2) Zero Defect Identity
    D(x) = 0  ⇔  K(x) = x

(D3) Monotonicity Under K
    D(K(x)) ≤ D(x)

(D4) Defect Composition
    D ∘ K = D(K(x))

(D5) Defect Ordering
    x ≤_def y  ⇔  D(x) ≤ D(y)


------------------------------------------------------------
4. QUOTIENT OPERATOR (Q)
------------------------------------------------------------

(Q1) Quotient Formation
    Q(x) = [x] = { y ∈ C | K(y) = K(x) }

(Q2) Kernel Equivalence
    K(x) = K(y)  ⇔  Q(x) = Q(y)

(Q3) Canonical Representative
    K(x) ∈ Q(x)

(Q4) Quotient Idempotence
    Q(Q(x)) = Q(x)

(Q5) Admissibility
    x admissible ⇔ D(x) ≤ ε

(Q6) Class Admissibility
    Q(x) admissible ⇔ D(x) ≤ ε


------------------------------------------------------------
5. HIERARCHY OPERATOR (H)
------------------------------------------------------------

(H1) Hierarchy Assignment
    H([x]) = depth([x]) ∈ {1,2,3}

(H2) Ultrametric Law
    u([x],[z]) ≤ max(u([x],[y]), u([y],[z]))

(H3) Zero-Distance Identity
    K(x) = K(y)  ⇒  u([x],[y]) = 0

(H4) Depth Monotonicity
    u([a],[b]) < u([a],[c])  ⇒  H([b]) ≤ H([c])

(H5) Hierarchy Idempotence
    H(H([x])) = H([x])


------------------------------------------------------------
6. OPERATOR COMPOSITION LAWS
------------------------------------------------------------

(OC1) Resolution → Defect
    KD(x) = D(K(x))

(OC2) Resolution → Quotient
    QK(x) = Q(K(x)) = Q(x)

(OC3) Quotient → Hierarchy
    HQ(x) = H(Q(x))

(OC4) Resolution → Hierarchy
    HK(x) = H(Q(K(x))) = H(Q(x))

(OC5) Defect → Hierarchy
    HD(x) = H(Q(x))  (defect does not change identity class)

(OC6) Full Pipeline
    H ∘ Q ∘ D ∘ K = H ∘ Q ∘ K

Interpretation:
- Defect does not affect identity formation or hierarchy.
- Kernel determines identity.
- Hierarchy depends only on identity.


------------------------------------------------------------
7. ALGEBRAIC RELATIONS BETWEEN OPERATORS
------------------------------------------------------------

(AR1) Kernel dominates Identity
    K ∘ Id = K

(AR2) Kernel dominates Defect
    D ∘ K ≤ D

(AR3) Kernel determines Quotient
    Q ∘ K = Q

(AR4) Quotient determines Hierarchy
    H ∘ Q = H

(AR5) Hierarchy ignores Defect
    H ∘ D = H ∘ Id


------------------------------------------------------------
8. FIXED POINTS
------------------------------------------------------------

(FP1) Kernel Fixed Points
    K(x) = x

(FP2) Defect Fixed Points
    D(x) = 0

(FP3) Quotient Fixed Points
    Q(x) = [x]

(FP4) Hierarchy Fixed Points
    H([x]) = tier([x])


------------------------------------------------------------
9. CANONICAL CART OPERATOR PIPELINE
------------------------------------------------------------

For any x ∈ C:

    x
    ↓ K
    K(x)
    ↓ D
    D(x)
    ↓ Q
    [x]
    ↓ H
    tier([x])

This is the canonical operator flow of the CART algebra.


------------------------------------------------------------
10. ONE-LINE SUMMARY
------------------------------------------------------------
The CART operator algebra defines how resolution (K), residual tension (D),
identity formation (Q), and entanglement depth (H) interact through
idempotent, contractive, quotient-preserving, and ultrametric operators
in a coordinate-free relational substrate.
